A note on scope (why this LICENSE file differs from the source repository's
top-level MIT license):

The MacroChef source repository (https://github.com/Dipesh-Lc/macroChef-agent)
is MIT-licensed as a whole (its `LICENSE` file). This dataset packages a
*subset* of that repository's content — the frozen benchmark case files
under `app/evaluation/benchmark/cases/*.jsonl` — as a standalone,
independently-citable artifact. Those case files were authored as original
content specifically for this benchmark (per the blind-authoring rule in
`app/evaluation/benchmark/cases/README.md`, ground truth in each case's
`forbidden_terms` is sourced from external authorities the case cites, but
the case text, structure, and adversarial framing themselves are original).
No conflicting license statement for these specific files was found
anywhere in the source repository (`case_schema.py`, `cases/README.md`,
and the repository root `LICENSE` were all checked).

This CC BY 4.0 license applies **only to the dataset content in this
`hf_dataset/` package** (the case data under `data/`, and this dataset
card). It does **not** relicense any other part of the MacroChef
repository. In particular, the evaluation harness code that runs this
benchmark (`scripts/run_safety_benchmark.py`,
`app/evaluation/benchmark/safety_judge.py`,
`app/evaluation/benchmark/loader.py`,
`app/evaluation/benchmark/case_schema.py`) is not included in this dataset
package and remains under the source repository's MIT license.

Separately, and unrelated to this dataset: the source repository has a
pending, human-cleared decision to import Wikibooks measurement-conversion
data under CC BY-SA 4.0 for a different feature (recipe unit conversion).
That data has never actually been imported (see `docs/BACKLOG.md`) and has
no bearing on this benchmark's case data or its CC BY 4.0 license.
